.TH SYS_SPAWN 2
.SH NAME
sys_spawn \- start a program (SYS_SPAWN)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_spawn(const void *elf, size_t len, const char *args,
               int uid, int gid, const mk_spawn_t *o);
long spawn_file(const char *path, const char *args, int uid, int gid,
                const mk_spawn_t *o);
long run(const char *path, const char *args);

typedef struct {
    int32_t flags, parent;    /* parent: with SPAWN_PARENT */
    mk_groups_t groups;       /* with SPAWN_GROUPS */
    char console[16];         /* with SPAWN_CONSOLE */
    uint64_t quota;           /* with SPAWN_QUOTA, bytes */
} mk_spawn_t;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_spawn
starts a new process from the 64-bit ELF executable of
.I len
bytes (at most 256 MiB) at
.I elf
in the caller's memory: its segments are copied into a new address
space, with a 32 KiB stack. It gets
.I args
("name arg1 arg2", at most 255 characters) as its first argument; its
name is the first word. It is the caller's child, on the caller's
terminal.
.PP
Its identity is the caller's; only root may choose another
.I uid
and
.I gid
(-1, -1: the caller's), which is how accounts start a user's shell. Root
starting another user gives it no groups unless
.B SPAWN_GROUPS
says.
.I o
(or 0) adds options in
.IR o\->flags :
.TP
.B SPAWN_GROUPS
(root) The child's groups.
.TP
.B SPAWN_PARENT
(root) The child's parent is
.IR o\->parent ,
which gets its end, not the caller.
.TP
.B SPAWN_DRIVER
(supervisor) Device rights: I/O ports, interrupts, device memory, DMA.
.TP
.B SPAWN_MODULE
(supervisor)
.I elf
is 0,
.I len
a boot module's number
.RB ( sys_modules (2)).
.TP
.B SPAWN_CONSOLE
The child's terminal,
.I o\->console
("port" or "port#channel"); else its parent's.
.TP
.B SPAWN_QUOTA
The most memory it may use, in bytes (pages it uses, DMA included).
Without it a child gets its parent's limit; nobody may give a child more
than its own.
.TP
.B SPAWN_BACKGROUND
Background work: its threads prefer E-cores. A child of a background
process is one too.
.TP
.B SPAWN_PGROUP
The child leads a new process group (its pid), which its own children
join: a shell's job, for
.B kill
of -pid (Ctrl-C). Without it, a child is in its parent's group.
.TP
.B SPAWN_SHARE
.I elf
lies, page-aligned, in a shared memory object the caller has mapped
.RB ( sys_shm_create (2)):
its read-only segments are mapped from the object's pages, not copied,
so processes started from one image share its code.
.PP
The library's
.B spawn_file
reads the program from the disk first (and passes the current folder);
.B run
starts it and waits for its status.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The child's pid, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
An option the caller may not give.
.TP
.B EINVAL
Not an executable for this processor,
.I args
too long,
.I len
above 256 MiB, a bad group count, or a quota of 0.
.TP
.B ENOENT
.RB ( SPAWN_MODULE )
No such boot module.
.TP
.B ESRCH
.RB ( SPAWN_PARENT )
No such parent, or it is ending.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
No memory, or the quota is too small for the program.
.TP
.B EFAULT
A pointer or the image is not readable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_wait (2),
.BR sys_exit (2),
.BR kill (2),
.BR sys_ident (2),
.BR sh (1)
